1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,680
 You know, make sure you spend some time after our service

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:07,760
 and say hi to each other, OK?

3
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:09,040
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

4
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:19,480
 And I was in Toronto for the past two weeks

5
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:21,840
 speaking in churches.

6
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:23,200
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:29,920
 So it's very good to be back to Chicago to our church.

8
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:32,720
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

9
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:35,680
 Now, as you can see, I have been thinking--

10
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:38,800
 we're thinking about women in ministry in this sermon series.

11
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:44,120
 So today, we are on one passage, which is called Romans 16.

12
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:45,360
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

13
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:53,920
 So thinking about women in ministry

14
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:56,920
 and how the Bible view women, before we do that,

15
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:59,080
 we have to reveal something.

16
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:01,760
 Some of the following propositions

17
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,880
 that I find meaningful for us.

18
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:08,880
 So we're going to read each one of them, all together five,

19
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:13,760
 and decide in your mind whether it is true or false.

20
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:14,920
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

21
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:26,360
 So I think about women in the Bible and women in ministry.

22
00:01:26,360 --> 00:01:30,880
 Number one, the Bible is a product of a patriarchal

23
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:32,240
 society.

24
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:35,520
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

25
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:37,400
 And true or false, right?

26
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:42,360
 The Bible, cultural background of the Bible, is patriarchal.

27
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:44,800
 It means no male is always on top, right?

28
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:46,440
 The men are on top.

29
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:48,800
 And the men are the leaders.

30
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:50,360
 But be careful.

31
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:54,880
 Even the Bible assumes a patriarchal system.

32
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:58,400
 That is not the same as the Bible endorsing it.

33
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:02,960
 It is assuming it, but it's not necessarily approving it.

34
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:04,120
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

35
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:20,000
 So number two is that the Bible is pretty much

36
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,960
 a male-centric literature, right?

37
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:23,840
 It's male-centric.

38
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:28,200
 It's coming through male eyes.

39
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:32,640
 You know, most of the authors, or all the authors, were men.

40
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:34,680
 And the language defaults to male.

41
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:38,680
 And you think about the Bible story, most of the heroes

42
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:44,080
 that we hear all the time, the genealogies or the backgrounds,

43
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:46,560
 they are always man-centered, right?

44
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:49,840
 So when a female reader reads the Bible

45
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:54,400
 and finds it hard for herself to plug into it--

46
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:58,120
 Moses, David, and Paul, they're all men--

47
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:00,800
 that feeling is real.

48
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:01,960
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

49
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:27,280
 So number one is true.

50
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:28,040
 Number two is true.

51
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:33,760
 And number three, the Bible challenges patriarchal values.

52
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:36,440
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

53
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:40,320
 Now, you expect that a book like the Bible coming out

54
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:44,120
 of a male-centered, male-first culture

55
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:45,880
 would despise women, right?

56
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:47,080
 You would expect that.

57
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:48,880
 Because it's a male book, right?

58
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:50,040
 It's a man's book.

59
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:52,600
 But the Bible does the opposite from time to time,

60
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:54,400
 and actually many times.

61
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:57,440
 It questions male-first values.

62
00:03:57,440 --> 00:04:00,360
 And sometimes it turns around and challenges

63
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:01,920
 the men themselves.

64
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:03,320
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

65
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:21,800
 So and then number four, women in the Bible

66
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:24,720
 play a strategic role in the biblical plan,

67
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:26,400
 the biblical history.

68
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:27,920
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

69
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:32,920
 And you think about the turning points,

70
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:36,240
 the important turning points in the Bible story.

71
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:38,560
 The Exodus, you have the midwife saving

72
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:42,480
 the child of the children of the Hebrews.

73
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:44,720
 And then Deborah, the judge.

74
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:48,600
 And then Ruth, Hannah, and all these women,

75
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:52,080
 they step in as strategic point of the plan of salvation

76
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:54,440
 so that the plan can move forward.

77
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:55,800
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

78
00:04:55,800 --> 00:05:13,040
 So number five, we must, we, as readers of the Bible,

79
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:17,200
 we must read the Bible scripture with high sensitivity

80
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:19,360
 to its context.

81
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:20,760
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

82
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:25,840
 Now, if you read the Bible out of the context,

83
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:29,360
 without the context, the Bible becomes a blank check for you,

84
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:30,000
 right?

85
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
 It will say whatever you want it to say.

86
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:34,280
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

87
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:42,840
 So we have gone through one, two, three, four, five, right?

88
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:46,360
 They are all true, or some of them are false?

89
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:47,560
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

90
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:52,920
 So they're all true, right?

91
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:53,880
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

92
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:54,840
 You agree?

93
00:05:54,840 --> 00:05:55,800
 Yeah, they're all true.

94
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:56,800
 They're all true.

95
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:58,000
 They're all true.

96
00:05:58,000 --> 00:05:58,600
 OK?

97
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:01,200
 So the Bible is a product of patriarchal society.

98
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:03,520
 It is a male-centric literature.

99
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:07,600
 Yet, it challenges values of the patriarchal society.

100
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:09,960
 And then women play important strategic role

101
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:11,640
 in the plan of salvation.

102
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:15,840
 And then, we must read the Bible within its own context.

103
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:17,200
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

104
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:37,480
 So today, we come to a very interesting passage, Romans 16.

105
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:40,560
 And you remember the letter of Romans.

106
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:43,040
 Paul finished this letter to the Roman Christians.

107
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:45,880
 And at the end, he sends some greetings.

108
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:48,360
 His greetings to a long list of people.

109
00:06:48,360 --> 00:06:49,640
 And you try to count them.

110
00:06:49,640 --> 00:06:51,560
 There were a lot of people being named.

111
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:53,640
 All of them, like, more than 20 names.

112
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:57,320
 But half of them were female.

113
00:06:57,320 --> 00:06:58,600
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

114
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:20,520
 So let's go through some of the names here.

115
00:07:20,520 --> 00:07:22,040
 The first point I want to point out

116
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:24,120
 is that, as we read this passage,

117
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:26,640
 we have to rethink our own assumptions.

118
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:27,840
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

119
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:35,600
 So Romans 16.7, it says, you know,

120
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:37,640
 the long list of people, Paul trying

121
00:07:37,640 --> 00:07:39,320
 to greet at the end of the letter.

122
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:43,820
 He says, "Greet Andrew Nicus and Julia.

123
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:46,280
 They are outstanding among the apostles,

124
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:49,840
 and they were in Christ before I was."

125
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:51,040
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

126
00:07:51,040 --> 00:08:04,080
 So first question I have for you is Julia.

127
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:06,440
 Is it a female name or a male name?

128
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:07,440
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

129
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:12,000
 Well, let me give you an easier question.

130
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,360
 OK, is Mark-- my name is Mark.

131
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:16,640
 Is Mark a male name or a female name?

132
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:17,600
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

133
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:21,720
 Male, right?

134
00:08:21,720 --> 00:08:22,440
 Yeah, thank you.

135
00:08:22,440 --> 00:08:24,400
 Yeah, male name, OK?

136
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:26,320
 Definitely a male name, OK?

137
00:08:26,320 --> 00:08:30,320
 Julia is actually a name for a woman, OK?

138
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:33,400
 But Paul is saying that this woman, Julia,

139
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:37,400
 is an outstanding apostle among all the apostles,

140
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:40,520
 not known to the apostles, but she

141
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:44,520
 is counted as one apostle, a prominent one.

142
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:45,680
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

143
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:56,960
 But it's very interesting.

144
00:08:56,960 --> 00:09:00,520
 In the long history of Bible translation, for centuries,

145
00:09:00,520 --> 00:09:05,120
 that female name got changed from Julia

146
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:08,040
 and quietly changed to Julius.

147
00:09:08,040 --> 00:09:10,840
 At an S, you become a male name.

148
00:09:10,840 --> 00:09:12,640
 And this is a problem, right?

149
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:15,160
 Because from among all the literatures

150
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:16,920
 we find in first century, there's

151
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:24,080
 no evidence that Julius was ever a male name.

152
00:09:24,080 --> 00:09:26,680
 And it appears that the translation,

153
00:09:26,680 --> 00:09:29,760
 as interpretations, trying to reinvent it.

154
00:09:29,760 --> 00:09:32,480
 Because along the line, maybe the translator

155
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:39,560
 assumed that a woman could not possibly be called an apostle.

156
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:42,560
 So the text must be wrong, right?

157
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:44,600
 But the modern critical translations

158
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:46,840
 have restored-- you know, we're trying

159
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:49,800
 to restore this name, Julia, a woman.

160
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:50,760
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

161
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:27,160
 So let me note here, if you check the older English

162
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:30,160
 translation, it says Julius.

163
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:31,040
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

164
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:38,480
 But the newer, the newer updated translations,

165
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:42,280
 they all spell Julia without the S, a female name.

166
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:43,360
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

167
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:49,760
 Now you sit with that.

168
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:53,360
 You know, the Bible didn't change.

169
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:54,400
 The Bible didn't change.

170
00:10:54,400 --> 00:10:55,200
 It didn't change.

171
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:56,960
 It never changed, OK?

172
00:10:56,960 --> 00:11:00,920
 It's the assumption of the translations

173
00:11:00,920 --> 00:11:03,200
 changed the verse.

174
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:08,800
 Someone was unsure, so sure that a woman cannot be an apostle.

175
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:15,480
 So they tried to edit the text so fit into their assumption.

176
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:16,560
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

177
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:32,280
 So the problem is not the Bible.

178
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:34,280
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

179
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:36,800
 The problem is us, Bible reader.

180
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:38,600
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

181
00:11:38,600 --> 00:11:41,880
 Now, so often, what we call what the Bible says--

182
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:43,160
 remember what the Bible says.

183
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:46,280
 You know, people tell you that's what the Bible says--

184
00:11:46,280 --> 00:11:51,440
 can actually be what we have been always been taught, right?

185
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:52,840
 Not actually what the Bible says,

186
00:11:52,840 --> 00:11:55,040
 but what we always been taught.

187
00:11:55,040 --> 00:11:56,600
 And those are not the same thing.

188
00:11:56,600 --> 00:11:58,680
 The two things are different.

189
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:03,760
 A tradition can be old, can be sincere, or deeply held,

190
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:08,560
 but still not carrying the same authority of Scripture itself.

191
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:09,920
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

192
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:31,880
 Now, we're thinking our own assumptions is not rebellion.

193
00:12:31,880 --> 00:12:32,440
 OK?

194
00:12:32,440 --> 00:12:33,760
 Take this, OK?

195
00:12:33,760 --> 00:12:34,760
 It's not rebellion.

196
00:12:34,760 --> 00:12:39,280
 It's not because we're anti-whatever establishment.

197
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:41,520
 It is actually the opposite.

198
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:45,920
 You know, we are going back to the Bible text with humility

199
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:49,560
 to let Bible says what it actually says.

200
00:12:49,560 --> 00:12:53,800
 And even when that overturns what we assumed before.

201
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:54,840
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

202
00:12:58,960 --> 00:13:14,680
 So number two-- point number two is that we have to recognize

203
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:17,240
 the value of women ministry.

204
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:18,160
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

205
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:22,560
 Yeah, so that's the second point.

206
00:13:22,560 --> 00:13:24,960
 First point is that we think our assumption.

207
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:27,640
 The second point is to recognize the value

208
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:29,760
 of the ministry of women.

209
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:30,920
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

210
00:13:30,920 --> 00:13:38,880
 So once our assumption is clear away,

211
00:13:38,880 --> 00:13:41,920
 we look at what's been on the page of the Bible

212
00:13:41,920 --> 00:13:43,000
 the whole time.

213
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:44,240
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

214
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:51,000
 So let's take a look at some of the verses here.

215
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:57,880
 Chapter 16 again, verse 3, it says, "Greet Priscilla

216
00:13:57,880 --> 00:13:59,560
 and Aquila."

217
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:01,040
 You know, there are two names here.

218
00:14:01,040 --> 00:14:03,360
 There's actually a couple.

219
00:14:03,360 --> 00:14:06,920
 Now, Priscilla is a female name or a male name.

220
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:07,760
 Female or male?

221
00:14:07,760 --> 00:14:08,800
 Priscilla.

222
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:11,560
 Anyone call Priscilla here?

223
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:12,240
 Female, right?

224
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:14,520
 It has actually female, right?

225
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:17,600
 Prisca or Priscilla, it's the same thing, right?

226
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:20,840
 Prisca or Priscilla, female name, right?

227
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:24,160
 There's a female name, Priscilla, and then Aquila.

228
00:14:24,160 --> 00:14:27,200
 Now, you notice that the wife's name

229
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:32,280
 is placed before the husband's name, right?

230
00:14:32,280 --> 00:14:33,280
 Before.

231
00:14:33,280 --> 00:14:37,080
 Now, in a male-centric world, that cannot be happening,

232
00:14:37,080 --> 00:14:38,040
 right?

233
00:14:38,040 --> 00:14:39,120
 That cannot be happening.

234
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:43,160
 Yet, you know, Paul is calling them co-workers.

235
00:14:43,160 --> 00:14:46,160
 Not helpers, but co-workers with him.

236
00:14:46,160 --> 00:15:09,200
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

237
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:11,920
 And then there are a strain of names, and I highlight them.

238
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:14,280
 Mary, of course, is a female name.

239
00:15:14,280 --> 00:15:16,600
 And then there are two more female names,

240
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:23,320
 Trivena and Trifosa and Persis, right?

241
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:25,120
 One, two, three, four, four names,

242
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:27,360
 they are all female names.

243
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:31,360
 And to each of the women, Paul attaches one word,

244
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:35,040
 and this says that they work hard for the kingdom.

245
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:35,880
 And here's the thing.

246
00:15:35,880 --> 00:15:39,600
 That's the same word Paul uses to describe his ministry,

247
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:45,520
 his apostolic ministry, his own sweat for the gospel.

248
00:15:45,520 --> 00:15:46,920
 So Paul is not being polite.

249
00:15:46,920 --> 00:15:49,920
 He's measuring their ministry, the women ministry,

250
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:53,880
 with the same word that he uses for his own ministry.

251
00:15:53,880 --> 00:16:22,000
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

252
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:25,560
 So you see that in this last chapter of the Book of Romans,

253
00:16:25,560 --> 00:16:29,760
 he recognizes, Paul recognizes, the value of women's ministry.

254
00:16:29,760 --> 00:16:32,440
 And it's not being generous, it's not just being kind.

255
00:16:32,440 --> 00:16:34,680
 It's simply just accuracy.

256
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:37,920
 Paul used the same vocabulary, co-worker, hard worker,

257
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:42,520
 apostle, to assign full weight, 100% weight, to the women

258
00:16:42,520 --> 00:16:43,960
 that he worked with.

259
00:16:43,960 --> 00:16:48,200
 And it's not just humility, it's just accuracy.

260
00:16:48,200 --> 00:16:50,120
 It's just telling the truth.

261
00:16:50,120 --> 00:17:15,480
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

262
00:17:15,480 --> 00:17:18,280
 So number three, not only we think our assumption,

263
00:17:18,280 --> 00:17:21,200
 not only we recognize the value of women ministry,

264
00:17:21,200 --> 00:17:23,640
 but third, we receive the ministry.

265
00:17:23,640 --> 00:17:27,600
 We receive the ministry of women fully.

266
00:17:27,600 --> 00:17:32,600
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

267
00:17:32,600 --> 00:17:35,880
 Now we come to the first name that Paul--

268
00:17:35,880 --> 00:17:38,520
 name in the last chapter.

269
00:17:38,520 --> 00:17:43,680
 And there's a reason why I want to save this name, the last.

270
00:17:43,680 --> 00:17:49,960
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

271
00:17:49,960 --> 00:17:54,120
 So verse 1, it says, Paul says to the churches in Rome,

272
00:17:54,120 --> 00:17:56,920
 he says, I command to you, our sister--

273
00:17:56,920 --> 00:17:58,920
 what is her name?

274
00:17:58,920 --> 00:17:59,920
 Phoebe, right?

275
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:00,760
 Phoebe.

276
00:18:00,760 --> 00:18:03,240
 Phoebe, a servant, or a deacon--

277
00:18:03,240 --> 00:18:06,960
 the original Greek word is deacon-- of the church.

278
00:18:06,960 --> 00:18:09,240
 And then receive her in the Lord in a way

279
00:18:09,240 --> 00:18:12,080
 that is worthy of the saints, and give her

280
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:14,440
 any help she may need, for she has

281
00:18:14,440 --> 00:18:17,840
 been a benefactor of many people,

282
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:21,040
 and benefactor of myself as well,

283
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:23,640
 or patron to myself as well.

284
00:18:23,640 --> 00:18:40,160
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

285
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:45,360
 Now, the first term Paul uses to describe Phoebe is deacon,

286
00:18:45,360 --> 00:18:48,200
 or translated English here is servant, right?

287
00:18:48,200 --> 00:18:49,840
 But actually, it's deacon, right?

288
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:51,240
 Deacon.

289
00:18:51,240 --> 00:18:56,480
 Not much less, not some lesser feminine title,

290
00:18:56,480 --> 00:19:01,400
 but exact same word he uses for male minister, male deacons,

291
00:19:01,400 --> 00:19:02,560
 and even for himself.

292
00:19:02,560 --> 00:19:03,960
 That's the same word.

293
00:19:03,960 --> 00:19:17,920
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

294
00:19:17,920 --> 00:19:21,000
 And then the second word is the word help, right?

295
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:24,440
 She has helped me, and here the English translation,

296
00:19:24,440 --> 00:19:28,720
 it says patron, which is correct, which is correct.

297
00:19:28,720 --> 00:19:32,240
 Because in the Roman society, patrons are the benefactors.

298
00:19:32,240 --> 00:19:33,520
 They are the protector.

299
00:19:33,520 --> 00:19:36,200
 They are the sponsor of some other people.

300
00:19:36,200 --> 00:19:43,480
 So being a patron is a person of standing and full of resources

301
00:19:43,480 --> 00:19:46,720
 to shelter other people, to lead other people.

302
00:19:46,720 --> 00:19:49,080
 So Phoebe is a patron.

303
00:19:49,080 --> 00:19:51,880
 She is not a servant or assistant.

304
00:19:51,880 --> 00:19:54,400
 She is a leader.

305
00:19:54,400 --> 00:20:21,440
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

306
00:20:21,440 --> 00:20:24,440
 And then there's this part that we often miss when we read

307
00:20:24,440 --> 00:20:27,100
 the Bible, especially this chapter.

308
00:20:27,100 --> 00:20:32,000
 Phoebe is certainly someone who carried this letter, which

309
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:38,680
 is the letter of Romans, right, from where she was to Rome,

310
00:20:38,680 --> 00:20:42,400
 because the letter was written to Roman Christians.

311
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:53,680
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

312
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:56,480
 Now, in the ancient world, it's different from us.

313
00:20:56,480 --> 00:21:00,320
 When we write a letter today, we want to send it out, right?

314
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:03,560
 Where do we go to send it out?

315
00:21:03,560 --> 00:21:07,320
 Post office, yes, exactly, USPS, as close as they are,

316
00:21:07,320 --> 00:21:09,320
 but they are still reliable, right?

317
00:21:09,320 --> 00:21:14,440
 We send a letter using USPS, right, or mail service.

318
00:21:14,440 --> 00:21:18,480
 But in the ancient world, there was no such kind like USPS,

319
00:21:18,480 --> 00:21:19,240
 right?

320
00:21:19,240 --> 00:21:23,360
 The letter carrier has to be the postman, right?

321
00:21:23,360 --> 00:21:27,120
 There has to be someone carry the letter from the sender

322
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:28,880
 to the audience, right?

323
00:21:28,880 --> 00:21:35,360
 So Phoebe is certainly the mail person, right?

324
00:21:35,360 --> 00:21:39,560
 So when the letter arrives and the church gather,

325
00:21:39,560 --> 00:21:42,960
 certainly the mail carrier, Phoebe herself,

326
00:21:42,960 --> 00:21:46,440
 is not just giving the letter to someone.

327
00:21:46,440 --> 00:21:48,820
 It's not like our modern postman.

328
00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:52,160
 She is going to stand up before the congregation

329
00:21:52,160 --> 00:21:54,400
 and read the letter aloud.

330
00:21:54,400 --> 00:21:58,160
 Phoebe, a female voice, reading the letter of Paul

331
00:21:58,160 --> 00:21:59,880
 aloud to the congregation.

332
00:21:59,880 --> 00:22:03,280
 And then in those contexts, she is actually

333
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:06,880
 interpreting, reading, and explaining,

334
00:22:06,880 --> 00:22:07,800
 answering questions.

335
00:22:07,800 --> 00:22:10,120
 What does Paul mean by this or that?

336
00:22:10,120 --> 00:22:12,560
 The carrier has to explain it.

337
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:15,000
 So the letter carrier was actually

338
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:20,200
 Paul's authorized preacher, authorized interpreter.

339
00:22:20,200 --> 00:22:50,120
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

340
00:22:50,120 --> 00:22:52,280
 So that's very interesting.

341
00:22:52,280 --> 00:22:55,480
 The very first person, the very first person

342
00:22:55,480 --> 00:22:59,360
 to publicly read and interpret and teach

343
00:22:59,360 --> 00:23:02,080
 the letter of Romans, the letter of Romans

344
00:23:02,080 --> 00:23:05,000
 is the most theologically profound letter

345
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,640
 found in all scripture, right?

346
00:23:07,640 --> 00:23:11,200
 The most important letter in the New Testament.

347
00:23:11,200 --> 00:23:13,320
 And the first person who preached

348
00:23:13,320 --> 00:23:16,760
 and the first person who read this letter to interpret it

349
00:23:16,760 --> 00:23:19,520
 was a woman.

350
00:23:19,520 --> 00:23:21,400
 Phoebe herself, right?

351
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:25,760
 So Paul chose Phoebe and sent Phoebe

352
00:23:25,760 --> 00:23:29,960
 to be her authorized interpreter or preacher.

353
00:23:29,960 --> 00:23:46,840
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

354
00:23:46,840 --> 00:23:49,920
 Now, that is really ironic here because some of the people

355
00:23:49,920 --> 00:23:52,880
 would quote the letters from Paul

356
00:23:52,880 --> 00:23:56,000
 to forbid women from teaching.

357
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:59,240
 But here, the first person, Phoebe herself,

358
00:23:59,240 --> 00:24:02,520
 ever teach one of the most important Paul's letters

359
00:24:02,520 --> 00:24:08,000
 was a woman and sent by a man, sent by Paul

360
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,480
 to read that letter to the church.

361
00:24:10,480 --> 00:24:24,280
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

362
00:24:24,280 --> 00:24:29,520
 So you have to notice Paul is not just instructing the church.

363
00:24:29,520 --> 00:24:31,840
 He is specifically telling them, you know,

364
00:24:31,840 --> 00:24:34,520
 you're not going to admire her.

365
00:24:34,520 --> 00:24:36,200
 You're not going to appreciate Phoebe,

366
00:24:36,200 --> 00:24:40,200
 but to receive her ministry, receive her teaching.

367
00:24:40,200 --> 00:24:45,600
 Give her what is needed for her and sit under her

368
00:24:45,600 --> 00:24:50,040
 as she carries the meaning of the letter to the church.

369
00:24:50,040 --> 00:25:02,080
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

370
00:25:02,080 --> 00:25:05,480
 So that's the last move here, the last point.

371
00:25:05,480 --> 00:25:11,960
 It's not just, you know, to respect a woman's ministry.

372
00:25:11,960 --> 00:25:13,480
 It's to receive it.

373
00:25:13,480 --> 00:25:15,400
 You know, it's another thing to receive it,

374
00:25:15,400 --> 00:25:19,720
 to let her read the letter, let her interpret the letter,

375
00:25:19,720 --> 00:25:22,960
 let her preach, and to sit under the ministry

376
00:25:22,960 --> 00:25:27,440
 of the Word of God that is actually given to Phoebe.

377
00:25:27,440 --> 00:25:44,560
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

378
00:25:44,560 --> 00:25:49,360
 So you see in Romans 16, the last chapter of the book,

379
00:25:49,360 --> 00:25:53,400
 Paul has a real team that have people working with him.

380
00:25:53,400 --> 00:25:56,480
 And on this team, there were deacons, there were patrons,

381
00:25:56,480 --> 00:26:00,720
 there were apostles, there were coworkers, one after another.

382
00:26:00,720 --> 00:26:05,240
 And you can tell many of them were women.

383
00:26:05,240 --> 00:26:19,240
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

384
00:26:19,240 --> 00:26:22,840
 So in the 1st century world, that was new.

385
00:26:22,840 --> 00:26:27,320
 That was something really new, because Paul worked side

386
00:26:27,320 --> 00:26:29,800
 by side with all these women.

387
00:26:29,800 --> 00:26:34,600
 And then if he hand Phoebe the longest letter,

388
00:26:34,600 --> 00:26:37,360
 the most profound letter he ever wrote,

389
00:26:37,360 --> 00:26:41,920
 and trusted Phoebe to read that and to interpret that.

390
00:26:41,920 --> 00:26:45,520
 And then he called Julia, an apostle,

391
00:26:45,520 --> 00:26:48,800
 and Priscilla, a prominent coworker,

392
00:26:48,800 --> 00:26:52,680
 and honored their labor out loud in this letter.

393
00:26:52,680 --> 00:27:12,480
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

394
00:27:12,480 --> 00:27:17,520
 Now, that's the way Paul treated sisters and coworkers.

395
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:23,000
 And that is the way our church should treat our female workers,

396
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:26,320
 the sisters, to honor them, to receive their ministries.

397
00:27:26,320 --> 00:27:33,240
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

398
00:27:33,240 --> 00:27:35,720
 And as I prepared this sermon, I thought

399
00:27:35,720 --> 00:27:39,680
 of a very important female minister in my life.

400
00:27:39,680 --> 00:27:47,580
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

401
00:27:47,580 --> 00:27:51,840
 When I was in college, I went to a church

402
00:27:51,840 --> 00:27:53,600
 with a college fellowship.

403
00:27:53,600 --> 00:27:57,040
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

404
00:27:57,040 --> 00:28:01,400
 And there's one female minister who has always been there.

405
00:28:01,400 --> 00:28:04,600
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

406
00:28:04,600 --> 00:28:08,320
 Now, she is not super knowledgeable.

407
00:28:08,320 --> 00:28:10,800
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

408
00:28:10,800 --> 00:28:13,280
 But she has always been faithful.

409
00:28:13,280 --> 00:28:15,840
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

410
00:28:15,840 --> 00:28:21,080
 Calling us young people, and keeping us accountable,

411
00:28:21,080 --> 00:28:22,640
 and praying for us.

412
00:28:22,640 --> 00:28:27,120
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

413
00:28:27,120 --> 00:28:32,040
 Without her, then I would not be able to enter ministry.

414
00:28:32,040 --> 00:28:38,440
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

415
00:28:38,440 --> 00:28:41,440
 Without her leadership, without her servanthood,

416
00:28:41,440 --> 00:28:45,320
 I would not be the person that I am now.

417
00:28:45,320 --> 00:28:49,840
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

418
00:28:49,840 --> 00:28:54,240
 So thank God that God raised women and men equally

419
00:28:54,240 --> 00:28:56,040
 to serve in his kingdom.

420
00:28:56,040 --> 00:29:09,200
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

421
00:29:09,200 --> 00:29:12,800
 So in the same way, our church is moving forward, right?

422
00:29:12,800 --> 00:29:16,160
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

423
00:29:16,160 --> 00:29:18,120
 Whether you're new, whether you're first time,

424
00:29:18,120 --> 00:29:20,200
 whether you've been here for a long, long time.

425
00:29:20,200 --> 00:29:24,160
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

426
00:29:24,160 --> 00:29:27,720
 God is calling all of us, each and every one of us.

427
00:29:27,720 --> 00:29:29,880
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

428
00:29:29,880 --> 00:29:32,000
 And calling us to co-work together,

429
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,920
 to build up his church, and to advance his kingdom.

430
00:29:34,920 --> 00:29:38,080
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

431
00:29:38,080 --> 00:29:41,400
 And of course, men and women.

432
00:29:41,400 --> 00:29:44,240
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

433
00:29:44,240 --> 00:29:45,560
 Well, shall we pray together?

434
00:29:45,560 --> 00:29:47,560
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

435
00:29:47,560 --> 00:29:50,080
 Dear Heavenly Father, we want to give you thanks.

436
00:29:50,080 --> 00:29:52,320
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

437
00:29:52,320 --> 00:29:55,840
 Because you raise up men and women for your kingdom.

438
00:29:55,840 --> 00:29:58,040
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

439
00:29:58,040 --> 00:30:03,000
 And you use men and women for your work.

440
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:06,240
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

441
00:30:06,240 --> 00:30:08,240
 And at this moment, we pray that you

442
00:30:08,240 --> 00:30:11,360
 will call each and every one of us.

443
00:30:11,360 --> 00:30:15,240
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

444
00:30:15,240 --> 00:30:17,840
 Call us to use our gifts.

445
00:30:17,840 --> 00:30:20,720
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

446
00:30:20,720 --> 00:30:22,440
 Use our resources.

447
00:30:22,440 --> 00:30:23,600
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

448
00:30:23,600 --> 00:30:24,760
 Use our lives.

449
00:30:24,760 --> 00:30:26,040
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

450
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:27,120
 To serve you.

451
00:30:27,120 --> 00:30:28,280
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

452
00:30:28,280 --> 00:30:29,920
 In Jesus' name, we pray.

453
00:30:29,920 --> 00:30:31,720
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

454
00:30:31,720 --> 00:30:32,320
 Amen.

455
00:30:32,320 --> 00:30:34,680
 [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

